A||N||16||Monkeyman||What do I do with  my headlights?||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||01-08-2000||05:11 PM||||I have to start driving my '86 2m4 to work even though it's not ready yet.  My headlight motors don't work so I'll just keep them up, but here's the problem.  I read that you are supposed to unplug a blue wire if you leave the headlights up.  I found a blue wire on the pass side and unplugged it.  I can't find one on the drivers side.  Where is it?  Also, if I leave the one plugged in on the pass side and turn the switch on, the motor comes on but nothing happens except for alot of noise.  (I assume that this means that I need a new gear?)  Absolutely nothing happens on the drivers side.  No noise or anything.  If all I need to get my headlights to go up/down is another gear, I'll buy it, but I don't want to spend lots of money for no reason.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Greg Piet||01-08-2000||08:54 PM||||Go here: <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/headlight.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/headlight.htm</A> <P>If you need gears, you can get bronze ones for about 50 bucks a pop from a guy in Florida (USA).  Unfortunately I forgot his name and number, but I have it somewhere.  I think a corvette headlight motor gear works (ecklers corvette sells them).  <BR>I just went to the junkyard, and found some motors that worked, then took them apart and cleaned them.  I bought a bronze gear, but havent installed it yet, but it looks really nice!<p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 01-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||02:58 AM||||Monkey, <P>First off, for right now just remove your entire motor assembly.  I have my headlights propped up with quarts of Castrol Syntech.  When I had to replace my 87's I used an empty bottle of my fiancee's Pantene.  My 87's were like that for over a month and my 85's have been like this for quite some time.   I haven't received my gears from Chris Cook yet.  He only got the check less than a week ago.  His are the most affordable at $35. but you know that.  I will let you know what I think about the quality when I receive them.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||3
Z||000003||dogtired||01-09-2000||03:30 AM||||Monkeyman<BR>Look around a little more there is a blue wire on both sides. Of course you may have had a creative mechanic?? That did something different???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>William, you do know the 84 thru 86 are the same and 87, 88 are the same but different from the 84 thru 86???   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Had problems with my 86GT and it turned out to be RELAYS (I still HATE Relays) Now on my 87 it was the little white bushing things inside the headlight gear assy. Got mine new ones from Rodney Dickman, Great quality stuff!!<BR>Just some thoughts  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Animals R US!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000004||Monkeyman||01-09-2000||06:12 AM||||William--Definately let me know about Chris Cooks gears.  I'm sure a gear would help on the pass side where I'm obviouly getting power, but what about the other side?  (And have I heard that you can just take the gear out and reverse it?)  I must not be getting any power to the other side.  What would cause that?  <P>Greg--I wish I could find some cheap working headlight motors in a boneyard.  By the time I get there, the motors are all gone.<P>Fellow Animal--Good thought about a "creative mechanic", but everything on the drivers side seems to be very factory.  There is a blue wire, but it doesn't look like it can be disconnected.  The blue wire on the pass side had a in line plug.<P>What would I look for on the dr side to see if it's getting power.  Maybe it works just fine, but no power is the only problem? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||01-09-2000||01:59 PM||||I think I got it all figured out (unfortunately).  The drivers side didn't have the relay in it at all (I must need new glasses to have missed it).  I took the relay from the pass side, transferred it to the drivers side and it worked...sort of.  Now either side that the relay is plugged into will go up or down as it should but...neither motor will shut off at the up or down limit.  It/they just sit and spin (which is exactly what I've been saying to my car for the past 30 minutes!).  Does this just mean that I need new gears in my motors?  (And a new relay for the drivers side, of course.)  Or could it be something else?  Oh, and with the relay removed, the lights turn on and off just like they're supposed to with no motor involved.  How much is a new relay (I'm afraid to ask)?||||reg||8
Z||000006||theogre||01-09-2000||02:20 PM||||in your case it sounds like the jel coupler in the center of the drive gears is gone. The gears themselves may still be good.<P>You'll need to pull the motors apart. I'm told you can reconstruct the jel coupler with silicone sealer. I haven't tried it tho.||||reg||1
Z||000007||862M4inCA||01-09-2000||02:35 PM||||Monkeyman, As I remember when calling for replacement relays on mine, Autozone wanted $14 each. You may want to double check that since I was calling on both the isolation, and the actuator relays. But it wouldn't hurt to call your local parts store and see what they have to say.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||01-09-2000||02:43 PM||||Thanx, ogre.  That would be great if that was all it was.  I just found a site that explains how to fix this with hot glue or silicone sealant.  I'll have to try it this week.  <P>Anyone got a working head light relay they don't need?||||reg||1
